namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page maths_page Mathematical background

  These pages describe the mathematics behind the methods implemented in %Ponca: what is minimized, how the solutions
  are obtained, what the computed quantities mean, and how they behave. They complement the \ref user_manual_page,
  which explains how to use the library, and the scientific publications listed in the \ref citelist "Bibliography".
  They assume a graduate level in linear algebra and calculus; the notions of differential geometry are recalled when
  needed.

  \section maths_contents Contents

  - \subpage maths_weights_page &mdash; neighborhoods, weight kernels and their derivatives.
  - \subpage maths_covariance_page &mdash; barycenter and covariance, plane and line fitting, covariance descriptors,
    and their behavior on smooth surfaces.
  - \subpage maths_spheres_page &mdash; algebraic spheres, the three sphere fitting procedures, and the Growing Least
    Squares descriptors.
  - \subpage maths_mls_page &mdash; Moving Least Squares surfaces and their projection operators.
  - \subpage maths_derivatives_page &mdash; differentiation of the fits with respect to the scale and to the position.
  - \subpage maths_curvature_page &mdash; curvature tensors: Monge patches, normal derivatives, Corrected Normal Current.
  - \subpage maths_spatial_page &mdash; kd-trees and neighbor graphs.

  \section maths_notations Notations

  | Symbol                                                           | Meaning |
  |------------------------------------------------------------------|---------|
  | \f$d\f$                                                          | dimension of the ambient space (`DataPoint::Dim`) |
  | \f$\mathbf{q}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d\f$, \f$\mathbf{n}_i\f$          | position and unit normal of the \f$i\f$-th point |
  | \f$\mathbf{p}\f$, \f$t > 0\f$                                    | evaluation position and scale, given to the neighbor filter |
  | \f$\mathbf{x}_i = \mathbf{q}_i - \mathbf{p}\f$                   | local coordinates of the \f$i\f$-th point (centered neighborhood frame) |
  | \f$w_i \geq 0\f$                                                 | weight of the \f$i\f$-th point, \f$W = \sum_i w_i\f$ |
  | \f$\langle f \rangle = \frac{1}{W} \sum_i w_i f_i\f$             | weighted mean of a quantity \f$f_i\f$ over the neighborhood |
  | \f$s : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}\f$                            | scalar field of an implicit primitive (its *potential*), whose zero set is the fitted surface |
  | \f$\nabla s\f$                                                   | gradient of the scalar field (`primitiveGradient`) |
  | \f$\lambda_0 \leq \dots \leq \lambda_{d-1}\f$, \f$\mathbf{v}_k\f$ | eigenvalues and unit eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix |
  | \f$k_{\min} \leq k_{\max}\f$, \f$H\f$, \f$K\f$                   | principal, mean and Gaussian curvatures |

  Sums run over the points with a positive weight. Unless stated otherwise, the fits are expressed in local
  coordinates, so that the evaluation position is the origin: this is how %Ponca stores them (see
  \ref fitting_frames).

  \section maths_conventions Conventions

  - **Hypersurfaces.** The fitted primitives are hypersurfaces of \f$\mathbb{R}^d\f$ (curves in 2D, surfaces in 3D);
    most fitting procedures work in any dimension, while the curvature tensors are defined in 3D.
  - **Orientation.** Oriented methods define scalar fields that are positive on the side the normals point to.
    Unoriented methods (covariance, algebraic and unoriented sphere fits) produce fields whose sign is arbitrary.
  - **Curvature sign.** For oriented estimators, curvatures are positive when the surface bends away from its normal:
    a sphere of radius \f$r\f$ with outward normals has curvature \f$1/r\f$. The conventions of each estimator are
    detailed in \ref maths_curvature_sign.

  \section maths_figures Figures

  The figures of these pages are generated by the Python scripts of `doc/scripts/` (requires NumPy and Matplotlib):
  \code{.sh}
  python doc/scripts/generate_figures.py
  \endcode
  The scripts rely on `doc/scripts/ponca_numpy.py`, which re-implements the formulas of these pages in a few lines of
  NumPy each, independently of the C++ library. Excerpts of this file are shown in the pages, as executable
  counterparts of the formulas. The generated images are stored in `doc/images/maths/`.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
